[Music]  this is a CBS News special report I've  turned to copal in New York the Labor  Department's newest weekly report on  jobless claims shows the coronavirus is  continuing to hurt the US economy the  last two reports were historic they  showed around 10 million Americans had  filed for unemployment that is more jobs  lost in a matter of two weeks than in  the entire 18 months of the Great  Recession the new report out this  warning shows 6.6 million additional  Americans filed jobless claims in the  prior week that matches the week before  which was a record CBS News business  analyst Jill Schlesinger joins us to dig  through these numbers Jill good morning  so we were hearing estimates of up to 7  million we're in a little bit under that  is that good news or was just as  catastrophic as the past two weeks I  think it's terrible news across the  board and now that we see these numbers  in broad daylight they even look worse  for these three weeks now I just want to  put this in context during the entire  recovery from the Great Recession the US  economy added about 20 million jobs we  are nearing those job losses just in  three or four weeks so this is a  staggering report the previous week was  revised a little bit higher so even  worse for the previous week the records  continued to just blow the minds of  every economist and every American who  sees the numbers come in some of whom  are feeling it day to day in their lives  yeah when economists review these these  numbers they use terms like  mind-boggling the bonkers I mean very  unscientific terms these are  once-in-a-generation  figures retail restaurants hospitality  those have been the industry's hit  hardest first where is this crisis going  from here well I think the good news is  that when we kind of push forward we  were likely we will likely see the  tapering off of these numbers what we  don't know is just how long people are  going to be sidelined it's such a  strange thing about the health pandemic  because in order to get to this route  cause of the financial problem we've got  to cure the health problem to cure the  health problem businesses have to  shutter we have to stay home that makes  these numbers these economic reports  terrible but we really must do this in  order to safely see the return of  workers to their payrolls I'm hopeful  that some of the Small Business  Administration loads will bring some of  these folks who are sidelined back onto  their payrolls they won't go to work but  at least they'll be getting paid during  this time unemployment is supposed to  cushion a period like this for Americans  who need it and in fact the federal  government has added to that cushion  $600 a week but we hear reports of  people struggling to get access to those  benefits what's the holdup  well these systems that are run through  the states we're not built to process 15  16 million people over the course of  just three weeks there's also the  problem that many states have these  legacy mainframe technology systems that  have not been kept up to date you know  it's like anything else when times are  going well you don't invest in the  things you don't think you need the  technology that supports the state  unemployment systems they're old and  they need help and hopefully when the  government adds money to state coffers  this will be an area where we see some  improvement but right now it is  incredibly frustrating for those who are  trying to get these much-needed  unemployment benefits all right Jill  Schlesinger thank you very much and this  is not a repeat six million additional  Americans lost their jobs in the past  week according to new numbers out just  this morning our coverage will continue  on our 24-hour streaming network cbsn  you can watch it at CBS news.com or on  the CBS News app there will be more to  come on your local news on the CBS  station and of course tonight on the CBS  Evening News with Norah O'Donnell many  of you will now return to CBS this  morning this has been a CBS News special  report  I'm Tony - Koppel CBS News New York

